{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Foundations of Interactive Multimedia Learning", "title": "Cognitive Architecture for Multimedia Learning", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting cognitive architecture for multimedia learning to cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_foundations_of_interactive_multimedia_learning", "ail606_supplement_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 1, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "01", "section": "01", "prereq_section_ids": [], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_1_1_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_1_1_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_1_1_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_1_1_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning_calibration"]}, "content": "# Cognitive Architecture for Multimedia Learning\n\n![Cognitive Architecture for Multimedia Learning textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/01-01-cognitive-architecture-for-multimedia-lear.png)\n\n<youtube-embed id=\"KbzmM30NXNQ\" title=\"Cognitive Load Theory: An Introduction\" caption=\"Watch after the opening visual. Use it to compare intrinsic, extraneous, and generative load with the diagnosis table you revise in this section.\"></youtube-embed>\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nEvery choice you make as a multimedia designer, where to put a label, whether to narrate or print a sentence, how fast a frame advances, is ultimately a bet about what the learner's mind can hold and process at that instant. If your mental model of that mind is vague, your design decisions become matters of taste. This section gives you the model that the rest of AIL 606 runs on: the human cognitive architecture proposed by Mayer and by Sweller, with its three memory stores and two sensory channels. The artifact you build against it, a cognitive load diagnosis table separating intrinsic, extraneous, and germane demand, is the instrument that turns \"this looks busy\" into \"this frame pushes seven interacting elements through one channel for a novice.\"\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Map any multimedia frame onto the dual-channel, limited-capacity, active-processing architecture.\n- Separate intrinsic, extraneous, and germane load and assign each a source in the artifact.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA designer opens a finished water-cycle screen: a clean animation on the left, a tidy paragraph on the right, brand colors throughout. It looks professional. But \"professional\" is not a load claim. The screen says nothing about whether a novice can follow it, and the diagnosis table is still blank where it should read which channel each element enters and how many pieces must be juggled at once. The case is not \"make it prettier.\" It is \"decode what this screen demands of working memory, then revise the demand.\"\n\n## Core Concept\n\nMultimedia learning theory rests on three empirical assumptions about the human mind, and the whole diagnosis table is just these three assumptions made operational.\n\n1. **Dual channels.** People process visual/pictorial material and auditory/verbal material through partially separate systems (Paivio's dual coding, Baddeley's working memory). Printed text is a special case: it enters through the eyes, so it loads the visual channel even though it is verbal in nature.\n2. **Limited capacity.** Each channel can hold only a few elements at once. For genuinely novel material a common working estimate is roughly four to seven interacting elements before the learner saturates and comprehension collapses.\n3. **Active processing.** Meaningful learning requires the learner to select relevant material, organize it into a coherent structure, and integrate it with prior knowledge in long-term memory. Passive exposure does not build a schema.\n\nFrom these assumptions Sweller distinguishes three demands the table must keep separate:\n\n| Load type | Source | Designer's job |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Intrinsic | Element interactivity of the content for *this* learner | Manage by sequencing, pre-training, segmenting; never delete the concept |\n| Extraneous | Poor presentation: split attention, redundancy, clutter | Reduce or remove |\n| Germane | Effort the learner spends building the schema | Protect and provoke |\n\nThe single most consequential move in the table is correctly attributing demand. \"Too hard\" is not a diagnosis; it conflates intrinsic with extraneous and licenses no specific fix. When you write a row, you are forced to ask: is this difficulty in the *material* or in the *presentation*?\n\nThe architecture treats each channel as a bounded counter of interacting elements. The explorer below lets you push verbal and visual elements through the two channels and watch the busiest channel approach a novice ceiling of roughly five elements. Notice that moving printed words to narration drains the visual channel without changing the total amount of content.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Channel load: routing elements through two channels\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"visual_pictorial\",\"label\":\"Visual elements (animation, diagram)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":8,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"el\",\"default\":3},{\"name\":\"printed_words\",\"label\":\"Printed-text elements (eyes / visual channel)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":8,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"el\",\"default\":4},{\"name\":\"narrated_words\",\"label\":\"Narrated-word elements (ears / auditory channel)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":8,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"el\",\"default\":0}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Visual channel load\",\"unit\":\"el\",\"expr\":\"visual_pictorial + printed_words\"},{\"label\":\"Auditory channel load\",\"unit\":\"el\",\"expr\":\"narrated_words\"},{\"label\":\"Headroom on busiest channel vs novice ceiling 5\",\"unit\":\"el\",\"expr\":\"5 - (visual_pictorial + printed_words)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"printed_words\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThe weak version below sounds plausible, but it does not yet give a reader enough evidence:\n\n> I made the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the channel, the element count, or which of the three loads moved. A stronger entry would read:\n\n> Frame 3 routed a 90-word paragraph and an animation through the same visual channel. I moved the paragraph to narration (auditory channel), which dropped the visual element count for a novice from roughly nine to four. The extraneous load fell; the intrinsic load (the water-cycle phases) was untouched.\n\nThe improvement is not longer wording. It names the channel, quantifies the element count, and states which load changed and why.\n\n### Annotated Before/After: Routing the Two Channels\n\nHere is one storyboard frame revised against the dual-channel and limited-capacity assumptions.\n\n**Before (frame 3).** On-screen: an animation of the water cycle plays while a 90-word printed paragraph describing evaporation, condensation, and precipitation sits beside it. Reader-action note: \"watch and read.\"\n\n1. Diagnosis: the animation and the 90-word paragraph both enter the visual channel, so they compete for the same limited capacity (estimated 5 to 7 interacting elements for a novice).\n2. Design decision: convert the printed paragraph to spoken narration (about 22 seconds) timed to the matching animation segment, and delete the on-screen paragraph.\n3. Rejected suggestion: an AI tool proposed keeping the paragraph and also adding a caption track. Rejected, because that adds a third visual-channel element and worsens the same overload.\n\n**After (frame 3).** On-screen: the same animation with brief signaling labels (3 words each) appearing as each phase begins; narration carries the explanation; no competing paragraph. Reader-action note: \"predict the next phase before it animates.\"\n\nThe improvement is not that the frame is prettier. The verbal load now travels through the auditory channel while the visual channel carries only the animation plus minimal labels, keeping the count of interacting elements within working-memory capacity, and the prediction prompt provokes the germane processing that builds the schema.\n\nWork the frame-3 revision one decision at a time, predicting each move before the reveal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Re-routing frame 3 across the two channels\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The animation and a 90-word printed paragraph both sit on screen. Which channel does the printed paragraph load?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The auditory channel, because the words are verbal\",\"The visual channel, because printed text enters through the eyes\",\"Neither, because text is not an element\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Printed text is verbal in content but enters through the eyes, so it loads the visual channel and competes with the animation for the same limited capacity.\"},{\"prompt\":\"To relieve the overload without deleting content, what is the governing move?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Shrink the font so the paragraph takes less space\",\"Convert the paragraph to timed narration so it travels through the unused auditory channel\",\"Add a caption track restating the narration\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Offloading the verbal stream to narration uses the idle auditory channel, dropping the visual element count for a novice from roughly nine to four while keeping all the content.\"},{\"reveal\":\"The intrinsic load (the water-cycle phases) is untouched; only extraneous competition fell. Adding a caption track would have reintroduced a third visual element, undoing the rebalancing, which is why that suggestion is rejected.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDiagnose before you decorate. For any frame, run the load triage in this order:\n\n1. **Channel audit:** List every element and the channel it enters. Two simultaneous demands in one channel is a split-attention or redundancy flag.\n2. **Capacity estimate:** Count interacting elements for the *target* learner, not for yourself. Novices interact more elements than experts because they lack the schema that chunks them.\n3. **Load attribution:** For each demand, is it intrinsic (in the content), extraneous (in the presentation), or germane (the productive struggle)? Cut extraneous, sequence intrinsic, protect germane.\n\nIf a revision lowers extraneous load but also strips a prediction or self-explanation prompt, you have traded away germane processing and probably made learning worse, not better.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is treating the architecture as a style guide (\"less text, more pictures\"). It is a capacity model. A picture-heavy frame can still overload a single channel, and a text-only frame can be fine for an expert whose schema chunks the words.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A frame shows a diagram plus on-screen narration captions that print exactly what the audio says. For a novice, is this dual-channel design?\" explanation=\"Captions are printed text, so they load the visual channel alongside the diagram while the audio is redundant. The verbal stream is duplicated across channels rather than split, so the visual channel is overloaded rather than relieved.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Yes, audio and text use two channels, so load is balanced\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No, the captions reload the visual channel and duplicate the audio, so the visual channel is still overloaded\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, more representations always reduce load\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n The second error is calling all difficulty \"load to be removed.\" Germane load is difficulty you want; an interface that removes the learner's need to predict, explain, or organize has lowered effort and lowered learning at the same time.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTake the screen from the opening case and run it through the architecture row by row. First, the elements: animation (visual), paragraph (visual, because printed text enters the eyes), brand banner (visual, irrelevant). Three demands, all in one channel, one of them extraneous. Limited capacity says a novice cannot hold the animation and a 90-word paragraph simultaneously, so the two compete and selection fails. The architecture predicts the fix before you reach for a tool: offload the verbal stream to the unused auditory channel. Notice what the model did not tell you to do, namely make it prettier. It told you to rebalance the channels and protect the one demand (predicting the next phase) that drives active processing. That is the difference between a design grounded in theory and a design grounded in preference.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead this section with the diagnosis table open, not your stylesheet. For each frame you encounter, write three lines: which channel each element loads, your element-count estimate for a novice, and the load type of each demand. A strong reader of cognitive architecture can predict where a learner will saturate before testing it, and can say which of the three assumptions, dual channels, limited capacity, or active processing, a given design choice serves or violates.\n\nA strong reader can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which channel does each element load, and do any compete?\n2. How many interacting elements does a novice face here, and where is the ceiling?\n3. Which difficulty is intrinsic, which extraneous, which germane?\n4. What would the element count become for an expert, and would the design still be justified?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe table is strong when every row attributes a demand to a channel and a load type, with an estimate a reviewer could dispute. \"Frame 3 is confusing\" is not a row. \"Frame 3 routes two verbal-visual elements through one channel; novice count about nine; extraneous, fixable by narration\" is a row. Treat brand polish as the last layer. If a row names a load but not its source, mark the gap and ask BigAL for a side-by-side of the same frame under a novice versus an expert capacity estimate, not a general definition of cognitive load.\n\nA useful final entry would read: \"I rebalanced frame 3 across channels because the visual count exceeded a novice's capacity. I rejected the caption-track suggestion because it reintroduced a third visual element and undid the rebalancing.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nNow move the same screen to an expert audience: graduate hydrologists reviewing the model for accuracy. The intrinsic load collapses, because their schema chunks the whole water cycle into one element, so the 90-word paragraph is no longer competing with much. The narration you added for novices may now feel slow and become extraneous itself (the expertise reversal effect). The architecture has not changed; the capacity estimate has, because the learner changed. If your only justification was \"narration is better than text,\" you cannot explain why the right answer flips. If your justification was a channel-and-capacity estimate for a named learner, you can.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/01-01-cognitive-architecture-for-multimedia-lear-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"01.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence for AIL 606 01.01. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that each row of the table names the channel an element loads, an element-count estimate for the target learner, the load type (intrinsic, extraneous, or germane), and the revision that followed, plus any suggestion you rejected and why.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how this section's ideas connect: the architecture grounds evidence-based revision of the artifact, and AI support is bounded by what the model can and cannot diagnose.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_1_1_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning", "stem": "A storyboard frame narrates an on-screen animation with a paragraph of printed text shown at the same time. Citing the dual-channel and limited-capacity assumptions of the cognitive theory of multimedia learning, which redesign is best justified?", "options": ["Keep the printed paragraph and add a third caption track so every learner has options.", "Move the explanation to spoken narration and delete the simultaneous printed paragraph so the visual channel is not overloaded by redundant text.", "Make the printed paragraph larger and bold so it is easier to read alongside the animation.", "Replace the animation with the same printed paragraph so only one channel is used."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Both the animation and the printed text load the visual channel, so they compete for limited visual working memory. Narrating the explanation routes it to the auditory channel, splitting the processing across both channels.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_1_cma_channels_are_modalities", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_1_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning", "stem": "In Mayer's model, what must happen for information to move from sensory memory into working memory?", "options": ["The learner must store it permanently in long-term memory first.", "The learner must select relevant words and images through attention before the rest decays.", "The system must present the same content in every modality simultaneously.", "The information transfers automatically because sensory memory has unlimited duration."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Sensory memory holds incoming material only briefly. The learner must actively select relevant words and images, or the material decays before it reaches the capacity-limited working memory.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_1_cma_unlimited_working_memory", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_1_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "stem": "A designer claims a screen is effective because it 'presents the content in both pictures and words.' Why is this not yet evidence of meaningful learning under the active-processing assumption?", "options": ["Because pictures should never be paired with words in any design.", "Because presenting in two formats does not by itself show the learner selected, organized, and integrated the material into a coherent mental model.", "Because the screen needs more decorative graphics to engage learners.", "Because all words should be spoken rather than printed in every case."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The active-processing assumption says learning requires the learner to build verbal and pictorial models and integrate them. Dual presentation is a necessary condition, not proof that integration occurred.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_1_cma_presentation_equals_learning", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_1_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning", "stem": "A long, expert-written narration accompanies a complex diagram with many interacting parts. Two novices report feeling overwhelmed. Mapping this to the three memory stores, where is the bottleneck and what redesign follows?", "options": ["The bottleneck is long-term memory; add a final summary slide to store everything.", "The bottleneck is limited working-memory capacity processing many interacting elements at once; segment the diagram into parts the learner advances through and give pacing control.", "The bottleneck is sensory memory; increase the font size of the narration transcript.", "There is no bottleneck; novices simply need to watch the narration twice."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Many interacting elements raise the load on capacity-limited working memory for novices who lack schemas. Segmenting and learner-paced advancement reduce the number of elements processed at once.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_1_cma_unlimited_working_memory", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_cognitive_architecture_for_multimedia_learning"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "1cf1306d441b41cf61331de5dd94c1b78dc6d49ad13f8c36c0ee924c124ebc2c", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_1_1_cma_channels_are_modalities", "pattern": "channel_modality_confusion", "description": "Believing printed text travels through the auditory/verbal channel because its content is verbal, so pairing an animation with on-screen text is treated as balancing the two channels.", "trigger": "learner pairs an animation with simultaneous on-screen text and assumes the two channels are balanced", "feedback": "Printed text enters through the eyes, so it loads the visual channel alongside the picture or animation, and the two compete for the same limited capacity. Route the explanation to spoken narration so the verbal stream travels through the idle auditory channel.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_1_cma_unlimited_working_memory", "pattern": "unlimited_working_memory", "description": "Assuming working memory can hold an arbitrary number of elements, so adding more on-screen content always helps.", "trigger": "learner adds detail or elements to a screen to be thorough and ignores capacity limits", "feedback": "Working memory processes only a few elements at once. Segment dense material and let the learner control pacing so the number of interacting elements stays within capacity.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_1_cma_presentation_equals_learning", "pattern": "presentation_equals_learning", "description": 